  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to lock onto a upper-level low \nforming over the Central Plains early Sunday associated with a \nnegatively tilted trough overhead and southeast winds, both \naloft and at the surface. Medium confidence (50-60%) exists of a\nsurface low pressure system south of the CWA (County Warning \nArea) on Sunday, but no front is expected. Meanwhile, a ridge \nstarts to build in over the Mountain West which is likely to \npropagate across our area Monday through Wednesday. Guidance \nshows winds aloft from the northwest which become southwesterly \nas said ridge moves through. Model output starts to become a \nlittle more fuzzy late Wednesday but most ensembles are picking \nup another negatively tilted trough with a strong upper-level \njet streak. This would theoretically bring stronger surface \nwinds as early as Tuesday, lasting through Wednesday night. \nGuidance projects moisture advection to take place early Sunday \nwith dew points forecast in the low to mid 40s.\n\nRain will be possible on Sunday morning as the low moves to the\nsouth of our CWA. PoPs are currently sitting around 20-40% \nthrough the afternoon hours. QPF is currently forecast somewhere\nin the range of 0.01 to 0.1 so at the very least some \naccumulating rainfall is probable. Chances for rain decrease \nsubstantially heading into Monday as that ridge takes control. \n5% or less PoPs are expected through Tuesday. Rainfall chances \npresent Wednesday as well with the aforementioned second trough.\nConfidence on any rain occurring a week from now is on the \nlower side at the moment with PoPs in the 15-25% range. \nNevertheless, will need to be monitored going into the weekend \nas ensembles sort out any uncertainties with this trough. Moving\nonto temperatures for our region, a warming trend is expected \nheading into next week as the ridge builds upon the Central \nCONUS. Highs in the mid to upper 40s should increase to the \nupper 50s/low 60s by Wednesday. Morning lows are anticipated to \nsit around freezing on Monday and Tuesday then increase a bit \ninto the 35-42 range on Wednesday.\n\nFire weather should not be a concern through early next week. \nDespite a recent dry spell, coupled with stronger stronger \nsurface winds forecasted for the Monday-Wednesday timeframe, \nRelative Humidities (RHs) are not likely to be low. It should be\non the wetter side with a system pulling in moisture from the \nsoutheast. RHs in the range of 50-80% during peak heating will \nbe optimal for keeping fire weather at bay. Wednesday will \nlikely be the driest day as temperatures warm and dewpoints \nremain stagnant but RHs shouldn't dip below 40%. As mentioned \nabove, surface winds pick up through the middle of next week as \na sustained 15-25 mph is forecasted. Gusts may reach 40 mph in \nsome areas of eastern Colorado.",
